WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i'm an olympic athlete  and i've followed a plant-based diet for  the last six years of my life  i don't eat meat and i don't eat eggs or  dairy  but it hasn't always been this way when  i first started competing  meat was a big part of my life  i competed as a lightweight rower in a  four-person boat  and i had to weigh 150 pounds just two  hours  before a mile and a half sprint  to give you some perspective as to how  much weight this is  i normally weighed 170 to 180 pounds  and i'm six feet two inches tall  so i had to lose anywhere from 20 to 30  pounds  before a rowing race  food and dieting were a big part of my  life then  and it still is today i want to talk to  you today about how i made food  a competitive advantage in my life and  how i could be in yours too  i hope to convince you that eating more  fruits and vegetables  and less meat will not only help you  grow faster  if that's what you're into but to think  quicker  to have more energy and most importantly  to live a longer  and healthier life  preparation for weigh-in started months  ahead of time  but the really intense part was the week  of racing  on a monday before friday race i started  weighing all my food with a little tiny  scale  three days before racing i'd drastically  reduce all salts and fibers from my diet  with six hours to go before the weigh-in  i stopped eating  and drinking completely finally with two  hours to go  i'd sweat in a hot bathtub  and weigh myself every 10 to 15 minutes  to make sure i didn't overdo it it was  an intense experience  that i don't recommend for anyone it  even sounds kind of crazy  saying out loud now  although making weight was important  leading up to race day  maintaining a healthy weight was an even  bigger struggle  while i was training i had to  consume an average of six to eight  thousand calories a day  just to make sure that i didn't lose any  weight while training  on a typical day before i stopped eating  meat  i would have five eggs for breakfast  a couple of sausages and greek yogurt  for lunch i'd have two giant hamburgers  with some bacon  and for dinner i'd have a t-bone steak  with about a pound of  rice and beans meat  was an important way to maintain my  weight  and to help me recover from all the  training that i did  or so i thought  in 2015 i failed to make the national  team  because of over training and because of  not recovering quickly enough  i was devastated i had been training  for the last 15 years to make the  olympics  i trained four to six hours a day seven  days a week  but the rest of the time i spent  thinking of how to get better at rowing  whether that was stretching watching  video  or eating but it wasn't happening  i wasn't getting better i was stuck  and i felt powerless  not making the team in 2015 made me  question everything about my approach to  training  including my approach to eating  i learned that food was not just a box  to be checked in my daily routine  but a tool that i could use as a  competitive advantage  the first lesson i learned in 2015 was  that food can make my life  a lot better  there's a new field in biotechnology  called inflammation and it theorizes  that  inflammation in the cells is what causes  most chronic heart disease  and chronic disease and what leads to a  shorter life expectancy  billions of dollars are spent a year on  drugs to reduce inflammation  in the cells but we don't have to wait  for these miracle drugs  fruits and vegetables already do this  a recent harvard public health study  found  that consuming five servings of fruits  and vegetables a day  can reduce your chances of chronic heart  disease and stroke  by 20  to put this number into perspective 20  percent  is comparable to the reduction that a  smoker gets  after 10 years of quitting smoking  the best part about the study is that  each additional  serving of fruits and vegetables that  you consume can reduce your chances of  chronic heart disease and stroke  by an additional four percent  to all the business school students out  there i can't think  of a better return on investment you can  literally  eat your way to better health  the second thing i learned in 2015 was  that food can make my life worse  remember all those t-bone steaks and  hamburgers that i was eating  each meal was making my blood more  viscous  it was making it harder for me to  recover from training  and for my heart to work during hard  workouts  think about the last big thanksgiving  meal that you had  or your trip to your favorite barbecue  joint  how did you feel i usually feel  tired and sleepy  over time this means plaque in your  arteries  it means a heart that can't work as well  for as long  the third thing i learned in 2015 was  that maintaining a good diet is hard  i don't need a scientific paper to tell  me that bacon tastes better than  broccoli  i know that i love bacon it's delicious  so how did i overcome this crave  i reduced the number of decisions that i  had to make  i prepared my meals on sundays for the  week and i ordered vegetarian pre-made  meals for the rest of the time  over time i didn't have any meat eggs or  dairy in my refrigerator  and i had no choice but to eat  what i had already planned  the second thing i learned to  maintaining a healthy diet  was to draw distinctions like i don't  eat red meat  or i don't eat dairy rather than picking  and choosing for every meal  the reason for this is again you narrow  your decisions  and you use less willpower over time  so the diet becomes more effective  and sustainable  because i don't eat meat some people  will call me  a vegetarian or a vegan but that's not  what i'm about  i don't want to tie my choices to a  better health to a certain identity  i think it's more important than that  i want to encourage you to be conscious  of the food choices that you make  and to use data to back up those  decisions  because whether we want to think about  them or not our food choices  are some of the most important decisions  that we'll make in life  thank you  you

